  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) In square-lattice iridates, enhanced higher-order spin interactions from spatially extended 5d orbitals and increased virtual charge fluctuations due to a small charge gap underlie strong deviation from the NN Heisenberg model; exchange symmetries and symmetry-distinct orders provide the context in which heterointerfacing different types of magnetism in superlattices may induce magnetic frustration through proximity coupling; enhanced long-range, multi-spin interactions competing with NN Heisenberg exchange can destabilize Néel AF order in favor of more exotic phases; spinon-dominated excitation continua are represented by broad continua in square-lattice antiferromagnets that have been attributed to spinon excitations; spinons are well-established quasiparticles in one-dimensional systems, while their presence in dimensions higher than one and in two-dimensional Heisenberg antiferromagnets remains contentious.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
